    "body": "<!-- claude-projects-attribution: {\"github_login\":\"siainbuletin\"} -->\n_Requested by **Sia** · [project thread](https://claude.ai/code/project/chan_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2?thread=cmsg_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2VvsAv9bMnVw5hmscGggixs)_\n\n## What\nAfter #152, `M-x` was fine at init but glitched again once Org was in use. The dimming remapped every face with a foreground (over a thousand, from `face-list`) in each buffer behind the posframe. Every redisplay of that buffer looks faces up in that remap list, so a busy Org buffer (indent, numbering, tables, tag groups) redraws slowly on each keystroke in the posframe.\n\n`sp-posframe-dim` now remaps `default` and the faces found on the visible text only (`sp-posframe--visible-faces`, a walk of the `face` property from `window-start` to `window-end`). The look is the same; text scrolled into view while the minibuffer is open is not dimmed.\n\nThe doc has two extra checks for the mirrored-buffer symptom: whether `vertico-buffer-mode` is on at the same time as the posframe, and `(setq sp-posframe-dim-always nil)` to rule the dimming out.\n\n## Not tested\nNothing was run in Emacs (none in the container). This is a suspected cause, not a confirmed one. I found nothing in the config that turns on `vertico-buffer-mode`, so I could not explain the mirrored buffer from the code.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01WWLNiBs9srTW9kUiGXduSF)_",
